\title{A Cubic Model of the Uncanny Valley: \\ Rigorous Monotonicity, Depth, and Recovery}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The \emph{uncanny valley}, introduced by Masahiro Mori in 1970, describes a
non-monotonic relationship between an artifact's human-likeness and an observer's
acceptance of it: acceptance rises with resemblance, peaks as the artifact becomes
nearly human, drops sharply into a trough of discomfort as the resemblance becomes
almost but not quite perfect, and finally recovers once the resemblance is
(near-)flawless. We show that this entire qualitative arc is captured exactly by
the elementary cubic $U(x) = x^3 - 3x$. Working from a single difference identity
$U(b) - U(a) = (b-a)(a^2 + ab + b^2 - 3)$ and two factorizations
$U(x) - 2 = (x-2)(x+1)^2$ and $U(x) + 2 = (x-1)^2(x+2)$, we establish strict
monotone ascent on $(-\infty, -1]$, strict monotone descent on $[-1, 1]$, strict
monotone recovery on $[1, \infty)$, a strict drop from the near-human peak
$U(-1) = 2$ to the valley bottom $U(1) = -2$, global minimality of the valley
bottom on $[-2, \infty)$, and full recovery whereby $U(x) > U(-1)$ for all $x > 2$.
These combine into a single uncanny-valley shape theorem. All arguments are
elementary, requiring only polynomial algebra and sign analysis.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The phenomenon}
In a short essay published in 1970, the roboticist Masahiro Mori proposed that as a
robot or artificial figure is made progressively more human-like, an observer's
emotional response does not increase monotonically. Instead, affinity climbs with
resemblance up to a point, then falls precipitously as the figure becomes almost --
but not entirely -- indistinguishable from a human, producing a sensation of
eeriness. Only when the resemblance becomes effectively perfect does affinity
recover. When affinity is plotted against human-likeness, the resulting dip is the
\emph{uncanny valley}.

The concept has been influential in robotics, animation, prosthetics, and computer
graphics, yet it is often regarded as intrinsically qualitative and resistant to
precise formalization. The purpose of this paper is to demonstrate the opposite:
that a fully rigorous, self-contained model of the uncanny valley exists, that it is
remarkably simple, and that every feature of Mori's curve can be proved from
elementary algebra.

\subsection{Contribution}
We isolate the acceptance curve $U(x) = x^3 - 3x$, where $x \in \mathbb{R}$ is a
signed measure of human-likeness and $U(x)$ is the observer's acceptance. We prove,
as a chain of mutually reinforcing results, that $U$ exhibits the complete
uncanny-valley profile: strict ascent on $(-\infty,-1]$, strict descent on
$[-1,1]$, strict recovery on $[1,\infty)$; landmark values $U(-1)=2$, $U(1)=-2$,
$U(2)=2$; a strict drop $U(1)<U(-1)$; global minimality of the valley bottom on
$[-2,\infty)$; full recovery $U(x)>U(-1)$ for $x>2$; and a capstone theorem
certifying the entire shape.

\section{The model and its foundational identity}

\begin{definition}[Acceptance curve]
The \emph{acceptance curve} is the function $U : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ defined
by $U(x) = x^3 - 3x$. Here $x$ denotes human-likeness on a signed scale and $U(x)$
denotes acceptance.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Difference identity]\label{thm:diff}
For all $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$,
\[
U(b) - U(a) = (b - a)\,\bigl(a^2 + ab + b^2 - 3\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expanding the right-hand side, $(b-a)(a^2 + ab + b^2) = b^3 - a^3$ and
$(b-a)(-3) = -3b + 3a$, so the right-hand side equals
$b^3 - a^3 - 3b + 3a = (b^3 - 3b) - (a^3 - 3a) = U(b) - U(a)$.
\end{proof}

The identity factors the change in acceptance into a direction term $(b-a)$ and a
symmetric quadratic term $Q(a,b) := a^2 + ab + b^2 - 3$. When $a < b$, the sign of
$U(b) - U(a)$ equals the sign of $Q(a,b)$.

\begin{lemma}[Peak factorization]\label{lem:peak}
For all $x$, $U(x) - 2 = (x - 2)(x + 1)^2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(x-2)(x+1)^2 = (x-2)(x^2 + 2x + 1) = x^3 - 3x - 2 = U(x) - 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Valley factorization]\label{lem:valley}
For all $x$, $U(x) + 2 = (x - 1)^2(x + 2)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(x-1)^2(x+2) = (x^2 - 2x + 1)(x + 2) = x^3 - 3x + 2 = U(x) + 2$.
\end{proof}

\section{The three landmark values}

\begin{proposition}[Landmarks]\label{prop:landmarks}
$U(-1) = 2$, \quad $U(1) = -2$, \quad $U(2) = 2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Direct substitution: $U(-1) = -1 + 3 = 2$; $U(1) = 1 - 3 = -2$; $U(2) = 8 - 6 = 2$.
\end{proof}

The point $x=-1$ is the near-human peak, $x=1$ is the valley bottom, and $x=2$ is
the recovery point where acceptance returns to the peak height.

\section{Monotonicity on the three regimes}

\begin{theorem}[Ascent]\label{thm:ascent}
If $a < b \le -1$, then $U(a) < U(b)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $a, b \le -1$ we have $a^2, b^2 \ge 1$ and $a + b \le -2$, so
$a^2 + ab + b^2 = \tfrac12\bigl((a+b)^2 + a^2 + b^2\bigr) \ge \tfrac12(4 + 2) = 3$,
with strict inequality as $a < b$. Hence $Q(a,b) > 0$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:diff}
and $b - a > 0$, $U(b) - U(a) = (b-a)Q(a,b) > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uncanny descent]\label{thm:descent}
If $-1 \le a < b \le 1$, then $U(b) < U(a)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $-1 \le a, b \le 1$ we have $a^2 \le 1$, $b^2 \le 1$, and $ab \le 1$, so
$a^2 + ab + b^2 \le 3$, with strict inequality since $a < b$ excludes the corner
cases. Hence $Q(a,b) < 0$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:diff} and $b - a > 0$,
$U(b) - U(a) = (b-a)Q(a,b) < 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Recovery]\label{thm:recovery}
If $1 \le a < b$, then $U(a) < U(b)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $a, b \ge 1$ we have $a^2 \ge 1$, $b^2 \ge 1$, $ab \ge 1$, so
$a^2 + ab + b^2 \ge 3$ with strict inequality as $a \ne b$. Hence $Q(a,b) > 0$, and
by Theorem~\ref{thm:diff} with $b - a > 0$, $U(b) - U(a) > 0$.
\end{proof}

\section{The uncanny valley, quantified}

\begin{theorem}[Strict drop]\label{thm:drop}
$U(1) < U(-1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:descent} with $a = -1$, $b = 1$: $U(1) < U(-1)$; numerically
$-2 < 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Immediate descent past the peak]\label{thm:below}
For every $x$ with $-1 < x \le 1$, $U(x) < U(-1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:descent} with $a = -1$ and $b = x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Global minimality on $[-2,\infty)$]\label{thm:min}
For every $x \ge -2$, $U(x) \ge U(1) = -2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:valley}, $U(x) + 2 = (x - 1)^2(x + 2)$. For $x \ge -2$ the factor
$x + 2 \ge 0$ and $(x-1)^2 \ge 0$, so $U(x) + 2 \ge 0$, i.e. $U(x) \ge -2 = U(1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Full recovery]\label{thm:full}
For every $x > 2$, $U(x) > U(-1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:peak}, $U(x) - 2 = (x - 2)(x + 1)^2$. For $x > 2$ the factor
$x - 2 > 0$ and $(x + 1)^2 > 0$, so $U(x) - 2 > 0$, i.e. $U(x) > 2 = U(-1)$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:landmarks}.
\end{proof}

\section{The capstone theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Uncanny-valley shape]\label{thm:shape}
There exist landmarks $x_0 = -1 < x_1 = 1 < x_2 = 3$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Ascent) for all $a < b \le x_0$, $U(a) < U(b)$;
\item (Descent) for all $x_0 \le a < b \le x_1$, $U(b) < U(a)$;
\item (Recovery) for all $x_1 \le a < b$, $U(a) < U(b)$;
\item (Drop) $U(x_1) < U(x_0)$;
\item (Overtaking) $U(x_0) < U(x_2)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $x_0 = -1$, $x_1 = 1$, $x_2 = 3$. Claims (1)--(3) are
Theorems~\ref{thm:ascent}, \ref{thm:descent}, \ref{thm:recovery}; claim (4) is
Theorem~\ref{thm:drop}; claim (5) follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:full} with $x = 3$.
\end{proof}

\section{Applications and discussion}

The model gives a minimal, closed-form target curve for acceptance as a function of
a realism parameter, with explicit valley bottom, peak, and recovery threshold.
More broadly, the analysis shows that the valley is the generic behavior of any
smooth quantity with exactly two interior turning points (a local maximum followed
by a local minimum), suggesting the phenomenon is not special to faces. Finally,
Theorem~\ref{thm:full} provides a quantitative rationale for persistence: crossing
the valley does not merely restore prior acceptance but strictly exceeds it.

Natural next steps include: the parametric family $U_c(x) = x^3 - 3c^2 x$; a
characterization of which monic cubics $x^3 + px + q$ have an uncanny valley (the
criterion is $p < 0$); a derivative-based reformulation via $U'(x) = 3x^2 - 3$; a
study of the uncanny gap $U(\text{peak}) - U(\text{valley})$; higher-degree curves
with multiple valleys and an alternation theorem; and smooth, non-polynomial
(sigmoid-minus-Gaussian) models proved to share the same qualitative shape.

\section{Conclusion}

The uncanny valley -- a phenomenon widely believed to be irreducibly qualitative --
is captured exactly by the cubic $U(x) = x^3 - 3x$. A single difference identity and
two factorizations yield the complete ascent $\to$ peak $\to$ drop $\to$ valley
$\to$ recovery $\to$ overtaking arc, with explicit landmark values, strict
monotonicity on all three regimes, global minimality of the valley bottom, and a
full-recovery theorem in which acceptance surpasses the original peak.

\end{document}

